+Prune families (`apply_keepset_family`) bin the rows once and prune every budget's keep-set
+in parallel waves on the `n_jobs` pool, largest keep-sets first. A wave holds at most as many
+candidates as the pool has threads, and few enough that their estimated row-proportional
+allocations fit in 1 GiB together. The estimate is conservative: per row, four bytes per raw
+feature (the full bank's cell map), two per model axis (a binned copy), four per kept main
+or pair (the cell-refit design, stored as `u16`), and 128 for the rebalance's working
+buffers at their peak (about 88 measured). A wave always
+holds one candidate, so a very large dataset prunes one at a time, and that single candidate
+may exceed the budget, as a lone `prune` call does. Each candidate's computation is
+unchanged, so every result equals an independent `prune(max_tables=…)`, and results keep the
+request order.
